#{[17592186565413 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186565367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565413 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565413 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565413 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨1,0,4⟩$ and $b=⟨2,-1,3⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=5$ and $f(b)=8,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565413 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14bd3999-4f71-5f69-b9ed-e9c986ba9f80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565413 #edb/ref 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 8-5 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨2,-1,3⟩-⟨1,0,4⟩) \\\\ 3 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ 3 & ={f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that ${f}_{x}(c)-{f}_{y}(c)-{f}_{z}(c)=3.$ *" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565434 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186565351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565434 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565434 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565434 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3⟩,$ respectively." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c3d7252-78a8-50a4-b514-2643c3b47ad3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565434 #edb/ref 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,3)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,3⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ ({1}^{2}+3)-({0}^{2}+1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ 3 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,1⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ 3 & =⟨2{c}_{1},1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ 3 & =2{c}_{1}+2 \\\\ 1 & =2{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{3-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{1-0} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2} & =x \\\\ y & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =2{c}_{1}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2},$ we obtain that ${c}_{2}=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1=2.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{1}{2},2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565461 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186565339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565461 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565461 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565461 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a function that is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where $a=⟨-1,-2,4⟩$ and $b=⟨3,-2,-5⟩$ are the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively. If $f(a)=-3$ and $f(b)=4,$ then what is missing from the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $\\text{_______________}\\,.$ *" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565461 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf45d7d2-b32a-596a-b136-67ab888373db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186565461 #edb/ref 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 4-(-3) & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨3,-2,-5⟩-⟨-1,-2,4⟩) \\\\ 7 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨4,0,-9⟩ \\\\ 7 & =4{f}_{x}(c)-9{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $4{f}_{x}(c)-9{f}_{z}(c)=7.$ *" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
